faqs:
  - q: "How does the Age Calculator determine exact age in years, months, and days?"
    a: "The calculator computes the calendar distance between your birth date and target date using UTC midnight timestamps to eliminate daylight saving time artifacts. It borrows days from the preceding month according to the actual month length (28 to 31 days) and accounts for leap year February 29th additions."
  - q: "How are leap year birthdays (February 29th) handled in common years?"
    a: "In common 365-day years, jurisdictions and cultures differ. The UK (Family Law Reform Act 1969) and Hong Kong recognize March 1st as the legal completion of the year, while jurisdictions like New Zealand, Taiwan, and several US states recognize February 28th. Our calculator provides a toggle allowing you to switch between both legal conventions."
  - q: "Can I calculate how old I will be on a future date?"
    a: "Yes. Change the 'Age at Date' field to any future date—such as a retirement date, visa application cutoff, or school start date—to view your exact age on that date."
  - q: "Why do lived hours differ from simply multiplying years by 8,760?"
    a: "A standard non-leap year has 8,760 hours (365 days × 24 hours), but leap years contain 8,784 hours (366 days × 24 hours). Our calculator sums the exact calendar days lived and multiplies by 24, accounting for every leap day that occurred throughout your life."
  - q: "Is my date of birth private and secure?"
    a: "Yes. All computations execute locally in your web browser client using pure JavaScript. Your dates are never sent to external servers or stored in any remote database."
  - q: "How does the tool calculate the countdown to my next birthday?"
    a: "The calculator determines your upcoming birthday in the current or subsequent calendar year, takes into account leap day rules if applicable, and computes the exact remaining days and day of the week."
  - q: "Can I calculate the age of a historical figure or event?"
    a: "Yes. Enter the event or birth date in the Date of Birth field and any subsequent date in the Target Date field to calculate exact historical lifespans or durations."
